*Legacy* (Don't use these)
```JS
getElementByID(id)
// Selects a single that has the provided ID
// Returns a single node.

getElementByClassName(name)
// Selects multiple nodes that have that class name
// Returns a nodeList

getElementByTagName(name)
// Selects multiple nodes that have the specified tag name
// Returns a nodeList 
```
Prior to 2012, developers where limited to the three above select methods. This introduces issues 
```HTML
<body>
	<p>
	<div> text 
		<p> 
		...
```
The `<p>` tag nested in the div is *not* the [[firstChild]], the text is.

*Post 2012* (Use these instead)
```JS
querySelector
// Selects a node that matches the provided CSS Selector
querySelectorAll
// Selects the Nodes (nodeList) that matches the provided CSS Selector

//Examples
const node = document.querySelector('#first div');
const nodes = document.querySelectorAll('.box');
```

Note: if you're tired of writing 
`querySelectorAll`
Just write a wrapper :) 
```JS
function selAll(selector) {
	return document.querySelectorAll(selector)
}

function sel(selector) {
	return document.querySelector(selector)
}
const nodes = selAll(.box);
```

**Example**
```html
<body>
	<div class=box>abc</div>
	<p class=box>cde</p>
	<article id=first>ghi</article>
	<p class=box>xyz</p>
</body>
```

```js
//I'm using the function wrappers above
const n1 = sel("#first");
console.log(n1.textContent) //Will display "ghi"

n1.innerHTML=`<em>Hello World!</em>`; //Changes the structure of the HTML
// BUT the <em> element doesn't get added to the DOM tree, which is a problem.
// This also has some security flaws! 

/* VERY COMMON ERROR */
const list = selAll(".box");
console.log(list.textContent);
//This diplays undefined, because nodeList doesn't have a textContent property.
//You need to iterate through the node list

for (let node of list) {
	console.log(node.textContent);
}

const list2 = selAll("wejirnfir");
//There are no "wejirnfir" selectors, so this will return an empty list.
```

**Example 2**
```html
